<speak>
<p>Witness. Field Season One. An interlude: The Price of Parts.</p>
<break time="800ms"/>
<p>A pause, between chapters.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p>You have now met a porpoise that drowns in nets set for another animal's swim bladder, and a rhino whose last cousin in Vietnam was shot for twenty centimeters of horn. Later in this season you will meet a leopard whose winter coat once sold for more than the people around it earned in a year. Three animals; oceans and forests apart; nothing in common — except the same machine humming behind all three.</p>
<p>This is the first of two pauses in Field Season One. No new animal here. Just a look at the machinery, because you cannot witness the counted few honestly without seeing what is counting them down.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>The machine.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p>The machine is old and simple: it turns an animal into parts, and parts into money. Its fuel is belief — that a dried swim bladder confers status, that ground horn confers health, that a spotted pelt confers rank. Belief sets a price, the price arms a weapon — a net, a rifle, a snare — and the weapon does not care what else it catches.</p>
<p>How big is the machine? Here the honest answer is: nobody can say precisely. Estimates for the illegal wildlife trade run from the single-digit billions to more than twenty billion dollars a year, and the United Nations' own 2024 crime report declines to put one number on it — what it documents instead is seizures touching one hundred and sixty-two countries and territories, and roughly four thousand species.</p>
<p>What the prices do to animals, you have already seen. A single dried totoaba bladder can bring tens of thousands of dollars per kilogram in its end market — while the fisher who sets the net is paid a small fraction, and the vaquita pays the rest. Rhino horn is keratin, the same protein as your fingernails. Its legend says sixty-five thousand dollars a kilogram — but investigators who measured real wholesale prices found less than a third of that, and as little as a tenth at the source. The gap between the legendary price and the real one is not a comfort. It is a lesson: the myth of the price is itself part of the machine, recruiting new hands with a promise it rarely keeps. And the demand has quietly changed shape — today's measured trade in horn is driven less by medicine than by luxury carvings. Status, not sickness.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>The limits.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p>Witness owes you the hard part. The main lever we have — ban it, then enforce the ban — has real limits, and the people fighting this trade say so themselves.</p>
<p>The UN's 2024 assessment is blunt: after two decades of concerted global action, wildlife trafficking has not been substantially reduced. Totoaba fishing has been illegal since 1975, its trade banned worldwide since 1977 — and the vaquita still fell to about ten animals, because a high enough price makes enforcement a dam that always leaks. Researchers add two more uncomfortable truths. A ban with no livelihood behind it lands hardest on the poorest link in the chain — the fisher and the hunter, not the broker. And scientists genuinely disagree about whether prohibition, or tightly regulated legal trade, protects species better. That debate is unresolved, and we will not pretend otherwise.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>What works — where the chain has broken.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p>One. Convict the traffickers, not just the poachers. In 2023, a Malaysian broker who moved some two hundred and nineteen kilograms of rhino horn was convicted in a United States federal court — one of the network men, not the man with the rifle. His sentence was eighteen months, which is its own honest data point about how lightly the law still weighs this crime.</p>
<p>Two. Make the treaty bite. In March 2023, the CITES treaty suspended all wildlife trade with Mexico over the vaquita's protection; within three weeks Mexico filed an acceptable action plan and the suspension lifted. A procedural win — a plan is not a net out of the water — but proof the treaty has teeth when it chooses to use them.</p>
<p>Three. Unmake the belief. The machine's first link is the softest. Demand-reduction campaigns work on the buyer's mind — horn is fingernail; the bladder cures nothing — because when the belief dies, the price dies with it.</p>
<p>Four. Take the profit out sideways. China's first totoaba-trafficking convictions came in 2018, with sentences of seven and eight years — and researchers are now testing whether legally farmed totoaba could undercut the black-market price entirely. An experiment, not a victory; we will report what it becomes.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>One question to carry with you. The machine runs on one idea: that a rare thing's value lives in its parts. Witnessing bets on the opposite — that its value lives in its being alive. Every purchase is a vote between those two ideas. Which one did your last one feed?</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>Every claim in this interlude is backed by a dated source, listed in the text. This narration uses a synthetic voice. Thank you for witnessing.</p>
</speak>
